problem","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":654.74934,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":400.6955041884412,"t":664.71198},"charspan":[0,70],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"Example 6.2. Consider the two-dimensional semilinear parabolic problem"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"∂u ∂t ( t, x, y ) -∂ 2 u ∂x 2 ( t, x, y ) -∂ 2 u ∂y 2 ( t, x, y ) = 1 1 + u ( t, x, y ) 2 + 1 1 + u ( t 2 -1 2 , x, y ) 2 , (6.63)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":616.9765375000001,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":106.56020000000001,"r":504.14487582135837,"t":645.0701575},"charspan":[0,130],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"for ( x, y ) ∈ [0 , 1] 2 and t ∈ [0 , 3], subject to homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. The initial condition is given by φ ( t, x, y ) = e -t x (1 -x ) y (1 -y ) for t ∈ [ -1 2 , 0].","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":582.5365375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.99966999999998,"r":503.99908128,"t":608.4702875000002},"charspan":[0,191],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"for ( x, y ) ∈ [0 , 1] 2 and t ∈ [0 , 3], subject to homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. The initial condition is given by φ ( t, x, y ) = e -t x (1 -x ) y (1 -y ) for t ∈ [ -1 2 , 0]."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"We apply standard finite differences with n = 200 grid points to discretize the problem in each spatial direction. In this example the exact solution is unknown. The reference solution is computed by the ERKC-C method with Gauss collocation points ( s = 3) using the constant step size h = 2 -11 . The errors of the ERKC-I and ERKC-C methods in the L ∞ (Ω) and the L 2 (Ω) norm at final time T = 3 are presented in Figures 3 and 4. The figures confirm the theoretical analysis. Moreover, a comparison of computational cost of the ERKC methods with Gauss collocation points ( s = 3) is presented in Table 1. For step sizes h = 2 -k with k = 3 , 4 , . . . , 9, the ERKC-I method consistently reduces the CPU time by approximately 12% compared to the ERKC-C method, demonstrating its computational efficiency across a range of step sizes.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":469.70922,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.99952999999996,"r":504.12712078567597,"t":575.3121600000001},"charspan":[0,835],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"We apply standard finite differences with n = 200 grid points to discretize the problem in each spatial direction. In this example the exact solution is unknown. The reference solution is computed by the ERKC-C method with Gauss collocation points ( s = 3) using the constant step size h = 2 -11 . The errors of the ERKC-I and ERKC-C methods in the L ∞ (Ω) and the L 2 (Ω) norm at final time T = 3 are presented in Figures 3 and 4. The figures confirm the theoretical analysis. Moreover, a comparison of computational cost of the ERKC methods with Gauss collocation points ( s = 3) is presented in Table 1. For step sizes h = 2 -k with k = 3 , 4 , . . . , 9, the ERKC-I method consistently reduces the CPU time by approximately 12% compared to the ERKC-C method, demonstrating its computational efficiency across a range of step sizes."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Figure 3: The convergence rates of ERKC-I methods (in the left panel) and ERKC-C methods (in the right panel) for (6.63). The errors are measured at T = 3 in the L ∞ (Ω) norm.","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":264.38933999999995,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":504.00875132737195,"t":286.35197999999997},"charspan":[0,175],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Figure 3: The convergence rates of ERKC-I methods (in the left panel) and ERKC-C methods (in the right panel) for (6.63). The errors are measured at T = 3 in the L ∞ (Ω) norm."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"